# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import Hot

@HotState struct State { var drawn: Int64 }

@c public func hot_frame(_ state: UnsafeMutableRawPointer,
                         _ frame: UnsafeMutableRawPointer) -> Int32

var host = try HotHost(image: "/data/hot/libframe.so", namespace: ns)
let result = host.frame(&hotFrame, now: Clock.monotonic())
```

# DESCRIPTION

A program that hot reloads is two parts: a host, which owns its loop, its windows and its files, and an image, a shared object holding the work of a frame.
All of the program's state lives in the state block, 1 MiB mapped at the same address, 128 GiB, in every run, so a pointer into it stays good across reloads.

The image declares its state type with `@HotState`, which makes it `BitwiseCopyable` (the compiler refuses a class, a closure or an existential in it, whose code would be the old image's), and exports its size and a hash of its layout as `hot_state_size` and `hot_state_hash`.
The hash covers the type's name and each stored property's name and type as written, in order: a field added, removed, retyped or moved changes it.
The image exports `hot_frame(state, frame)`, which the host calls once a frame with the state (the block past its 16-byte header) and a `HotFrame`: the pixels to draw (`pixels`, `width`, `height`, `stride` in pixels, 0x00RRGGBB), the frame's number, the time it is aimed at, and up to eight keys pressed since the last frame.
`HotFrame`'s `set` and `fill` draw.

`HotHost(image:namespace:)` maps the block and loads the image; it needs the VMEX resource a manifest's `reload` grants (manifest(6)), which only cpu images give.
`frame` calls the running image, then, at most every 50 ms, looks at the image's file (its qid, size and time); if it has changed, it loads a fresh copy: the file's bytes copied into new memory, made runnable, linked against the program's exports (it must be linked to export them) and placed at an address of its own.
The new copy runs from the next frame.
Old copies are never unloaded, so a string or a function an old image left in the state still works.
An image whose state hash isn't the block's is refused, and the old one runs on, unless it exports `hot_migrate(state, hash)`, which is given the block and the hash it holds and returns whether it took it.
`loads`, `lastLoad` (from the change seen to the new code ready) and `refused` say how it went.

A new image is written beside the old under another name and renamed over it, so the host never reads half a file: vx-reload(1) does that from the dev machine, through a cpu session.

minimal-hot(4) is minimal, the SDK's reference app, hot reloading its frame.

